{
  "abstract": "Let N >= 2 be an integer, let u_1 < u_2 < ... be positive integers with liminf u_{j+1}/u_j >= 2, and let lambda_j be nonzero rational numbers with log H(lambda_j) = o(u_j), where H(a/b) = max(|a|, b) in lowest terms. The series x = sum_j lambda_j N^{-u_j} converges absolutely, is irrational, and has irrationality exponent limsup u_{j+1}/u_j, finite or infinite. The coefficients may have arbitrary signs, and the boundary case liminf u_{j+1}/u_j = 2 is included, with ratios allowed to approach 2 from below. The proof compares continued-fraction denominator gaps of consecutive truncations. A perturbation lemma places each new gap in at least one of five classes: bounded, inherited, central, short, or reflected. The reflected class gives the estimate needed at the boundary ratio 2. This project formalizes the paper's main theorem in Lean 4 with Mathlib.",
